00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.670 align:start position:0%
 
well it was pretty clear from today's  pmq's that labour wants a clear dividing  line between itself and the government  over how to pay for this very expensive  new freeze on energy prices well we're  joined now by the shadow business  secretary jonathan reynolds thank you  very much for being  with us so what do you want to see  tomorrow from this trust well i do think  and we have had it trailed and there'll  be something to match labour's offer to  freeze bills i mean we've been quite  clear people cannot pay a household  energy bill of four or five thousand  pounds  now there are mixed messages and  different stories coming out from  government but uh what we would like to  see is the existing package of resources  the government's put there mushed with a  windfall tax believe it's legitimate and  fair to take some of that unprecedented  profit for the benefit of the nation as  a whole we'd also have benefit from  lower um inflation put that together and  that's how we would fund a six-month  freeze in energy prices and have support  for business as well that's what we want  to see now there are we don't know what  the package will be um clearly if it is  as one version of the story is that  households will bear all the cost of  that but over a longer period of time i  think that is unfair and that's a drag  on the economy the government will have  to consider and if it's borrowing well  what we've heard so far from  quasi-quattangle is trust is that they  believe they can cut taxes  increase spending and somehow that will  pay for itself i mean that is that's  clearly untrue we know it's untrue from  previous actions of former conservative  governments  it's interesting isn't it because you  obviously want to see  part of it funded through taxation and  you were talking there about you know  liz truster's plan just not making sense  but i guess what they would argue  is that the way uh to growth is through  lower taxes that's what she wants to see  isn't it and i guess if you're talking  about the you know the record  she would also argue i suppose that now  is the time to try something different  to do something new to not go through  the same tax and spend can you see the  argument  i know that's their argument but this is  where the battleground is going to be so  first of all um illustrious has been a  member of every government over the last  12 years she bears some responsibility  for that i think it is interesting that  their own analysis is that that has been  a failure i mean they're very clear on  that i know quasi quartens called that  treasury orthodoxy but for most of  that's conservative orthodoxy so we both  have the same analysis you know things  haven't been as they should be now for  us  the problem is fundamentally there's a  lack of growth lack of productivity in  the british economy low business  investment is a part of that we know  from what george osborne tried that  cutting the headline rate of corporation  tax did not increase business investment  we already tried that we know it to be  true rishi sonic to be fair to him made  this case and was correct in doing so  what we would say is that lack of growth  comes from lack of clear policy lack of  free consistency no industrial strategy  or it's been tried twice and dropped  twice and that's the wrong message to  send to business we don't spend enough  on science in this country our research  and development it is a much smaller  proportion of gdp than any other  developed nation these are the things to  look at and this belief that there's a  magic wand of cutting corporation tax  and you can pay for that as the  chancellor suggested on monday by what  they call supply side reforms that will  make the economy grow i'm afraid that  it's conservative code for cutting  employment rights and environmental  protections it has failed and it's wrong  we'll find out won't we i guess you know  whose argument is right uh in the months  ahead certainly and liz trust also wants  to uh look at energy supply and the  future of energy too and we're expecting  her of course to be  looking at things like more exploration  in the north sea at reuters reporting  this evening that she is poised to  announce more drilling licenses in the  north sea dozens apparently  more licenses for uh exploration in the  north sea  is that a good thing in your view well  we'll have to see the detail because  any transition plan for the uk has  always had gas as part of that at the  same time i understand like you want to  see the detail but but we know that this  is the road of traven travel that this  is what they want to go down she said it  before she wants to see more exploration  in the north sea is that something that  you would support well she'll have to  prove that it's consistent with our  climate change objectives  internationally agreed treaties that we  have signed up to you'll also have to  how do you do that though how do you  because we have a committee on climate  change that has a carbon budget you're  either in favor of exploration than also  you're not well it's a part of the  transition if  what we don't know is is this new  drilling for already uh found oil fields  gas fields or is it new exploration that  the time skills of that will determine  that there's also a suggestion of well i  mean i'm skeptical that that can be done  on a time scale consistent with the net  zero transition and if it's uh if if it  goes it gets so just to be clear if it  goes against the net zero targets then  you would be against  exploration because not only is that a  breach of our commitments by not  sticking to our commitments we raise the  risk the cost of capital for the kind of  investments we need in onshore wind  offshore wind solar new nuclear a  hydrogen industry you know we lose the  jobs of the future as well as breaching  things that we've signed up for and if  it's a return to fracking as we've heard  part of it look the best case scenario  for fracking even if people wanted it  and it's clear they don't with something  like four billion cubic meters in four  or five years time that is in no way  going to compensate for the loss of  russian supply to europe and quite  frankly i don't think even our geology  and environmental standards in this  country that could be done in a way so  it's the wrong answer and some people  would some people would say look uh we  understand of course the how important  the commitment to net zero is we  understand the uh need for investment in  renewables but in a time of energy  supply crisis don't we need to look at  every option on the table including in  the north sea including fracking you can  build a wind farm quicker than you can  get fossil fuels out of the north sea  should we be doing both of those things  well it depends if if she can prove that  is consistent with our net zero  objectives but if she can't not only is  that the wrong thing to do it will risk  the investments that we most need to see  for the long-term future of the country  and i think again you know there's an  element of of to be honest fantasy about  a lot of list dresses plans whether it's  on the economy in tax and spend or on  areas like this now we need the detail  to be fair to them but in terms of what  i've seen in the conservative leadership  contest i think we should be worried  there does seem uh one of the few things  that there is a consensus over is the  need to try and act to at least freeze  or reduce people's bills in the short  term  we also have a crisis in energy supply  is there a risk that by acting to reduce  bills or to keep bills lower than they  would be otherwise we're not  incentivizing people to cut their usage  at a time when it's energy supply that  is the issue i mean are you worried  about blackouts do we need to look about  rationing  i'm not worried by that because first of  all when people say freezing prices at  the current levels there's no incentive  to reduce your own consumption well  let's be clear that we're freezing them  at levels considerably higher so i you  know the incentive is certainly there  for people in my constituency and i  think the nation as a whole if the  government makes the right decisions  there shouldn't be the risk of what you  are saying but you know let's be clear  there hasn't been there's a huge drop  off in home insulation installations  after 2010 david cameron cut the green  crap that that you know reduced the  ambition of their own policy you could  have taken 15 percent you know roughly  out of the demand in terms of natural  gas had those decisions not been made  the bannon onshore wind has been  counterproductive and closing the  storage facility and you know again  another conservative decision really  poor decisions that have increased our  exposure but the government can still  make better decisions now and that's  what they need to do alongside measures  should we be looking at things like  they're looking at in germany like  stopping lighting public monuments or  asking shops to shut their doors well i  think there is a  there is some benefit in a public  education campaign around you know  different ways to reduce consumption an  amazing number of homes in this country  don't even have thermostats and that  could relatively easily be solved but i  would worry by talking about that that  we are only the government can make the  kind of intervention that is required on  a crisis of this scale and there's  always in us all living our lives in a  more efficient way and using scarce  resources as best as possible that's not  an answer to the crisis this requires  real action from government because  that's the scale of the challenge okay  interesting stuff thank you very much  jonathan reynolds

